---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';

// Every number, title, name and date on this page comes from src/data/facts.ts.
// Do not retype one here - npm run check:facts will fail the build.
---

<Layout
  title="Student Assignment Systems"
  description={`The logistics infrastructure behind experiential learning at ${facts.aisc.schoolShort} Chennai, assignment engines for SEVAI, Discover India, Discovery Day, and STEAM Week that placed ${facts.assignmentSystems.studentsPlaced} students into trips, activities, and workshops.`}
>
  <Nav />
  <main>

    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-navy via-teal-900 to-teal text-white py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
        <a href="/work" class="text-teal-200 text-sm hover:text-white transition-colors">← Back to all work</a>
        <span class="tag bg-white/10 text-white border border-white/20 mt-6 mb-5 inline-block">Systems · Google Apps Script + Sheets</span>
        <p class="text-amber text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Chapter 2 · Turn the solution into infrastructure</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-5 leading-tight">Student Assignment Systems</h1>
        <p class="text-gray-300 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
          The logistics infrastructure for experiential learning. Across four programs, I built the
          assignment engines that quietly place hundreds of students into the trips, activities, and
          workshops that make a school's signature experiences actually happen.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- METRICS BAR -->
    <section class="bg-teal text-white py-8">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
          {[
            { value: facts.assignmentSystems.studentsPlaced, label: 'Students placed' },
            { value: '4', label: 'Programs powered' },
            { value: facts.discoverIndia.trips, label: 'Discover India trips' },
            { value: facts.assignmentSystems.hoursSavedPerYear, label: 'Hours saved' },
          ].map(({ value, label }) => (
            <div>
              <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-amber">{value}</div>
              <div class="text-sm text-teal-100 mt-1">{label}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 space-y-14">

        <!-- OVERVIEW -->
        <div>
          <span class="tag mb-4">Overview</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">The infrastructure behind experiential learning</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Experiential programs, service learning, "Week Without Walls" trips, activity days, STEAM
              workshops, are the experiences students remember most. They're also a logistics nightmare:
              hundreds of students, ranked preferences, capacity caps, fairness constraints, and teacher
              staffing, all of which has to resolve into a clean assignment before anyone boards a bus.
            </p>
            <p>
              Over a single year I built and ran the assignment engines for four of {facts.aisc.schoolShort}'s flagship
              programs. Individually each saved real time; together they formed a reusable pattern for how
              the school runs experiential learning at scale.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <div>
          <span class="tag mb-4">The Problem</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Great programs, fragile logistics</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Every one of these programs was being assembled by hand, different spreadsheets, different
              people, different ad-hoc rules each time. The work was invisible until it broke: an
              over-capacity trip, a grade-skewed workshop, a student who never got placed. And because the
              process lived in someone's head, it couldn't be handed off or trusted to run the same way twice.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/assignment-systems-overview.png"
          alt="The four assignment engines side by side"
          caption="Four programmes, one pattern: ranked preference in, balanced placement out."
        />

        <!-- WHAT I BUILT -->
        <div>
          <span class="tag mb-4">What I Built</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Four assignment engines, one pattern</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">

            <div class="card border-l-4 border-teal">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-2">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-navy">SEVAI Community Service</h3>
                <span class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-medium">Oct–Nov 2025</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The system that started it all: a {facts.sevai.scriptLines} line engine assigning {facts.sevai.students} students to {facts.sevai.activities} activities
                by ranked preference, with grade balance, capacity limits, automated notifications, and
                test mode. Saved {facts.sevai.hoursSaved} hours per cycle.
                <a href="/work/sevai-assignment-system" class="text-teal font-medium hover:underline">Full case study →</a>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card border-l-4 border-amber">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-2">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-navy">Discover India, "Week Without Walls"</h3>
                <span class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-medium">Apr–May 2026</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The largest of the four. A complete assignment algorithm plus a {facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page operations manual
                to place {facts.discoverIndia.students} students across {facts.discoverIndia.trips} trips spanning three pillars, Adventure, Service, and
                Culture. Handled {facts.discoverIndia.signUps} student sign-up responses, capacity rules (20 students and 3 teachers
                per trip), gender balance, and a formal appeals process. I built the backend engine; I also
                chaperoned a trip myself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card border-l-4 border-teal">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-2">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-navy">Discovery Day 2026</h3>
                <span class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-medium">Feb 2026</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Automated assignment across {facts.discoveryDay.activities} activities, morning, afternoon, and full-day sessions, from {facts.discoveryDay.signUps} student sign-up responses, with a capacity-tracked dashboard and near-full
                assignment at launch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card border-l-4 border-amber">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-2">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-navy">STEAM Week 2026 Distribution</h3>
                <span class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-medium">Mar 2026</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An automated workshop-distribution tool allocating {facts.steamWeek.students} students across {facts.steamWeek.workshops} prototype
                workshops over three days for Grades 6–8. I owned the assignment-automation piece in
                partnership with the program leads.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- IMPACT -->
        <div>
          <span class="tag mb-4">Impact &amp; Results</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Hundreds of students, placed reliably</h2>
          <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
            {[
              { value: facts.assignmentSystems.studentsPlaced, label: 'Total placements', sub: 'across four programs' },
              { value: facts.discoverIndia.trips, label: 'Trips assigned', sub: 'Discover India alone' },
              { value: '34', label: 'Activities', sub: 'Discovery Day sessions' },
            ].map(({ value, label, sub }) => (
              <div class="text-center p-5 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl">
                <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-amber mb-1">{value}</div>
                <div class="font-semibold text-navy text-sm">{label}</div>
                <div class="text-gray-500 text-xs mt-1">{sub}</div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Across SEVAI, Discover India, Discovery Day, and STEAM Week, the systems placed well over
              700 students by ranked preference while enforcing capacity, grade, and gender balance, and they did it repeatably. Each program could re-run its assignment, preview before
              committing, and hand the process to someone new without losing the logic.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Discover India system in particular was built as a deliberate handover artifact:
              {facts.discoverIndia.manualPages} pages of documentation alongside the algorithm, so the school's signature trip program
              could keep running long after I left.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- LESSONS -->
        <div>
          <span class="tag mb-4">Lessons Learned</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">What I'd carry forward</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-4">
            {[
              ['Logistics is pedagogy’s silent partner.', 'The trip nobody remembers as "well-organized" is the one that worked. Good assignment infrastructure is invisible by design, and that’s the point.'],
              ['Reuse beats rebuild.', 'Solving SEVAI cleanly meant Discover India, Discovery Day, and STEAM Week each started from a proven pattern instead of a blank spreadsheet.'],
              ['Build the manual, not just the machine.', `A ${facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page operations manual is what turns a clever script into an institutional system that outlives its author.`],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li class="card">
                <p class="font-semibold text-navy mb-1" set:html={title}></p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed" set:html={desc}></p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="py-16 bg-navy text-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 text-center">
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-5">Running a program that's held together by spreadsheets and luck?</h2>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="/work" class="btn-secondary border-white/40 text-white hover:bg-white hover:text-teal">More Case Studies</a>
          <a href="/work-with-me#contact" class="btn-amber">Let's Talk</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
